WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.068
A busy payments partition takes about 260 gigabytes a day.

2
00:00:07.068 --> 00:00:10.457
After seven days, the oldest records must go.

3
00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:12.123
One big file won't do.

4
00:00:12.123 --> 00:00:21.325
A file system can cut a file short only at its end, so dropping the oldest day means copying the other one and a half terabytes, every day.

5
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:28.397
So the broker cuts the log into a chain of files called segments, each named after its first record's offset.

6
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.736
Only the newest, the active segment, takes appends.

7
00:00:31.736 --> 00:00:35.242
When it's full, the broker closes it and starts the next.

8
00:00:35.242 --> 00:00:37.025
Closed segments never change.

9
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:41.410
Retention lets records older than seven days go: the red ones.

10
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:47.337
But the broker deletes files, so a segment goes only when its newest record has expired.

11
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:51.000
Every five minutes it checks from the oldest.

12
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.464
Segments 0 and 104 have expired.

13
00:00:53.464 --> 00:00:57.239
208's newest record is fresh, so the check stops.

14
00:00:57.433 --> 00:01:03.733
Both files are deleted whole, and the first readable offset jumps from 0 to 208.

15
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:11.963
Records 208 to 249 are past seven days but still readable, since they share a file with fresh ones.

16
00:01:11.963 --> 00:01:13.909
Seven days is a minimum.

17
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:15.673
Some topics hold state.

18
00:01:15.673 --> 00:01:22.379
Each card-status record gives a card's latest status, and services replay them to rebuild a map.

19
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:26.467
Deleting by age would drop a card unchanged for three weeks.

20
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.937
You want the last record for each key.

21
00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:32.504
To delete a card, you append its key with no value.

22
00:01:32.504 --> 00:01:34.288
That record is a tombstone.

23
00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:41.001
Once the segment rolls, the log cleaner, a background thread, notes the offset of each key's last record.

24
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:44.347
Then it rewrites the segment, keeping only those.

25
00:01:44.347 --> 00:01:46.748
Offsets never change, so it has gaps.

26
00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:51.035
The tombstone stays a day, so every reader sees card-2 go.

27
00:01:51.035 --> 00:01:52.237
Then it goes too.

28
00:01:52.433 --> 00:01:58.819
Retention drops whole old segments, and compaction rewrites them to keep each key's last value.
